The exported [incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) describes one known bug. Its executable views tell a different story: the tenant's date order, booking form and report channel identify distinct problems. This review replaces that single-fix interpretation.

## What the reports support

<SplitSummary />

## Separate fixes

- **Parser:** the shared parser reads every date month first. For tenants whose locale writes day first, ambiguous dates swap day and month. Parse according to the tenant's date order, or use a structured date value rather than an ambiguous string. Test both day-first and month-first tenants and the app, email and booking-v2 form paths.
- **Hint:** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, while its form interprets entries according to a day-first tenant's locale. People following the displayed hint supply a value the form misreads. Make the hint, input example, validation and parser agree. Test the affected booking-v3 tenants, alongside booking-v2 and month-first controls.

Neither fix alone establishes that all reports are resolved. Intake's browser-derived tag is a guess; it does not replace the tenant directory. Unknown tenants stay unplaced for investigation.

## The queue records two different events

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

This snapshot was exported on 4 October 2026. It is not the live queue. Re-export before claiming current totals or resolution. These components deduplicate by report ID, filter ticket membership and recompute from the data supplied at the next build. Repeated subjects and overlapping export pages must not inflate the count.

## Triage by evidence

Read the report, resolve its tenant, inspect the booking form and channel, and compare causes before merging. A matching subject alone proves neither a shared cause nor a shared fix. When the tenant or cause is unknown, keep the report separate until investigated.

<DedupeExplorer />

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) is retained unchanged as history. [Proposed decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause) explains the replacement; the support desk must approve the operational policy.

## Evidence and boundaries

The report export, tenant directory, `causes.ts`, `locales.ts`, desk notes and executable views support this review. Report bodies are observations; tenant-based groups are the supplied desk classification. The timing anomalies need investigation, and no root cause or repair has been verified against production code.

